## Cue script syntax
Save the script as `episodes/<DATE>-<TYPE>/script.txt` (UTF-8, one cue per line; blank lines and `#` comments are ignored).
```
$example_lines
@speaker Jane Doe | f | guest               declare an extra voice (quote reader, clip, translated speaker)
@speaker Clip 1 | m | clip | en-GB-RyanNeural   optional explicit edge-tts voice
Jane Doe: text                              line by a declared speaker
[INTRO] [OUTRO]                             theme music: speech starts as the intro resolves; outro plays after the last line
[STING]                                     short musical transition between segments
[SWOOSH]                                    lighter whoosh transition (quick items, change of topic)
[BED ON] ... [BED OFF]                      soft music bed under the lines in between (ducked ~26 LU under voice)
[PAUSE 0.8]                                 silence in seconds
```
Prosody overrides after the speaker id: `(rate)`, `(rate,pitch)` or `(rate,pitch,volume)`, e.g. `(-3%)` for a serious
caveat or `(+2%,+1Hz)` for a light question. Keep them small (about ±8 %, ±3 Hz). The renderer also strips any
tag that ends up inside the text, so a tag is never read aloud.
Check every script before rendering: `render.py SHOW_DIR script.txt --check` (syntax, speakers, length, cue limits).

## Sourcing and honesty rules (always apply)
1. Never invent quotes, interviews, guests, listener mail, reactions or anecdotes. Quote someone only with their
   name and the outlet, word for word as published.
2. Every factual claim must trace to a source you read this run. List the sources in `episodes/<id>/sources.md`
   (title, outlet, URL, time read).
3. Keep fact and analysis apart. Attribute confirmed facts ("according to …") and label analysis as analysis.
4. Summarise in your own words. Do not read paywalled or copyrighted text out in full. A translation of someone
   else's show is for personal use only: say in the episode description that an AI translated and voiced it, and link the original.
5. Give dates and time zones explicitly. If something is unconfirmed or still developing, say so on air.
6. To fix a mistake, edit the script, re-render and re-publish with the same DATE-TYPE. The episode keeps its guid
   and pubDate.
7. Write for the ear: short sentences, names before pronouns, no tables, no URLs read aloud, numbers only when they
   matter (round them and explain what they mean).

